A pilgrimage is a gathering, and [[Org/The Harlequins/index|the Harlequins]] are known to organize them. The Party holds one stolen invitation to a pilgrimage dated for Saturday: blue paper reading that through connections, guile, or something perhaps more unsavory, the bearer has acquired an invitation, with the rest in gold text. It invites one and names no recipient.

[[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anaïs Marchal|Anaïs Marchal]] has been to a pilgrimage before, in [[Geography/Istria/index|Istria]], and offered to attend this one with his sister [[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anouk Marchal|Anouk]] separately from the Party and meet them there. The Party believes the gathering is connected to the six gods said to be coming to destroy [[Færrin]].

The pilgrimage the Party holds an invitation to takes place at [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]], a dead mining town a little under a day's drive out of [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]]. Its centerpiece is an Istrian strider under construction, a platform on eight legs with buildings on top, still ringed with scaffolding. The town's main street is dressed for a party: electric fairy lights on the old lamp posts, banners reading "Pilgrimage Welcome," tables of hors d'oeuvres and champagne, and hundreds of guests in masks of every kind, some with three or four armed attendants. Guests queue at a table to have their invitations checked and are handed masks. Crossing the town line flattens every raiment to the same plain gray, so that no one can be read for the god behind their blessing.

On the night of the gathering the guests queued at a table to hand in their invitations and were handed plain white masks, all alike, that pressed to any face and changed nothing of the wearer, and then walked the main street of [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]]. Along it stood a fortune-telling booth, a barker before a rainbow-prism box, a Harlequin with a painter's palette before blank wooden mannequins, a table of dice and poker chips, and two signposted paths out of town, one marked with a fairy ring of mushrooms and one with crossed swords. The [[Org/The Harlequins/index|Harlequins]] served drinks and hors d'oeuvres, and their platters carried a faint whiff of sulfur. Weapons were not taken from anyone.

The host of the evening, a tall lanky figure plainly not human, stood atop the unfinished strider and spoke over the crowd without raising his voice. He wore a black pinstriped suit and a plain white mask like the guests', with crescent eyes and a wide black toothless grin. He said they had all proved their worth by getting in, invited or not, and that he meant to take them on his pilgrimage to the Void Side Wall aboard the strider he and his Istrian companions were building, which he called the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]]. Since the strider could not hold the some four hundred guests, he said, they would play a game. He showed them a [[Phenomena/Keystone|keystone]] and set them to finding the other nineteen within six months. He said he would not harm anyone that night, since the strider was not finished, and then vanished in a flash of smoke.

The paths led to games. The crossed-swords path led to the disused train yard, where a Harlequin at an observation deck spun a wheel of colors and rang a bell, and creatures came out of the smoke to fight whoever went down. The Party fought the red and survived, and were led to a foreman's office and given three questions. They learned that the keystone nearest the [[Sin and Tonic]] is number 14, held by a [[Phenomena/Hearts|Heart]] calling itself Rapid Connection, and that [[People/Hogan Voss Darkmoor|Archmechanic Hogan Voss Darkmoor]] of the Darkmoors is the man most likely to lead them to it. The Harlequin would not give a keystone's location, nor cross streets to it, and dropped through a trapdoor when the questions were done.

The fairy-ring path led off the main street to a parking lot behind a shuttered store, where a Harlequin ran an association game: three mushroom rings set in a Venn diagram, one player to each, and items to be laid in the four overlaps that connect the players. What connected them earned a soft ding; what did not brought an anvil, a pit of acid or a volley of darts, and the players dodged. The Party won a question from it and were named winner twelve of the evening.

The main-street games paid in hints too. The fortune teller read the Party a Harrow fortune, the Fool for the past, the World Tree inverted for a broken connection, the Hooded Man for what was to come, and the Forest for the rising, and then gave them a hint. The barker before the rainbow prism set seven walls of seven colors around a key, with seven potions to pass them and a riddle to work out the order. The table of dice took a fifty-gold buy-in against a hint.

While they waited in the lines the Party fell in with two of the other guests, an elf and a gnome who had fought the green creatures in the train yard while the Party fought the red. The elf gave her name as [[Org/Protectorate/People/Tamara McKay|Tamara McKay]] of the [[Org/Protectorate/index|Protectorate]], and said the two of them were after keystone 8.

[[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anaïs Marchal|Anaïs]] and [[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anouk Marchal|Anouk]], who meant to sneak in on their own, were not among the guests the Party saw, and had not come back to the [[Sin and Tonic]] by the time the Party returned the next morning.
